- F-64 **The compiled checker refuses every type that an api declares, trait or object, that extends a trait whose `comprises` clause has `...`, whether the clause lists it or not (row 354: the refusal's message, its site and the way around).** The specification allows a listed extender there (F-171), so this is a defect of the checker. The team's `compiler_tests/XXX3q` pins the refusal, with a second test that row 354 names, so a fix that skips it for a listed type turns both red. No api-declared type of the one library extends `QQ`, which its api closes by `comprises { ... }` (F-63), so the refusal does not fall on it; it falls on `IntMap`'s api (row 357). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md`, "Defects and their homes".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md, "Defects and their homes"; note: pilot round 1, case 4: takes F-63's sentence on `QQ`; "So the form `comprises { T, ... }` cannot stand in an api that declares `T`" and "a component's types may extend such a trait" to row 354, which holds both (its claim, and its notes: the test of `isApi`, the way around); the specification's reading (an api's clause may hold `...` and list an extender; only a draft note refuses the api's own extender, and a release build leaves draft notes out) to F-171, the owner of the text's `comprises`; `XXX10p` to row 354's notes.

- F-171 **The specification reads a `comprises` clause as the restart's Types chapter does: every value of the trait is a value of a listed type.** So a trait or object that extends the closed trait must be below a listed type, with two exceptions. A trait with a `comprises` clause of its own may stay unlisted if each of its own listed types is below a listed type of the closed trait. A trait with static parameters may stay unlisted if at least one type extends it and every type that extends it is below a listed type. A listed type may be an instantiation at the trait's static parameters or `where`-clause variables. In an api, the clause may also hold `...` beside the extenders that it lists; only a draft note refuses the api's own declaration of an extender, and a release build leaves draft notes out (`Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations"). The text does not say whether a bare type variable may be listed; walk overflows its stack on one (row 407). The compiled checker departs in three ways: it checks only the closed trait's immediate subtypes (row 487), it reads the clause in the subtrait's own type-variable names (row 489), and it finds extenders by the simple text of a name (row 490). It also refuses every extender that an api declares of a trait whose clause has `...` (F-64). Whether the team's commented `comprises Integral[\I\] where [\I\]` is valid is open (F-121). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-comprises/decision-record.md` section 2; `Specification/basic/traits.tex:246-269`.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md, "Defects and their homes"; note: pilot round 1, case 4: takes F-64's reading of the text on `...` in an api's clause, word for word but for the order, and points to F-64 for the checker's refusal; the rest of the entry unchanged.

